313:## 22. A parse cannot overflow its stack (2026-11-02)
314-
315-Resolves [07](./07-edge-cases-and-failure-modes.md) Part 1 #2 and replaces §21's reasoning for the nesting guard.
316-
317-- **What was wrong.** §21 treated the bracket scan as the bound on the parser's recursion. It is not one. A quote inside a regex literal hid the rest of the file from it, and arrow functions, conditionals, generic types and JSX recurse without brackets. In the release CLI, 5,000 parentheses after `/'/`, 20,000 levels of `Array<`, `() =>` or `a ? b :`, or 30,000 nested `<div>` overflowed the 8 MiB main thread and aborted the whole search with no output. OXC 0.152 has no depth limit of its own.
318-- **Measured** (parse plus extraction, aarch64 macOS, stack per nesting level, optimized / unoptimized): `(` or `[` 1,562 / 3,241 B; `[` in a type 1,701 / 4,581 B, the most per source byte; `{a:` 1,847 / 3,636 B; `Array<` 1,562 / 3,436 B; `() =>` 802 / 965 B; `a ? b :` 704 / 676 B; `<div>` 464 / 1,218 B; `!` 80 / 561 B. Left-deep `a + b` and `a.b()` chains parse in a loop and cost 80 / 561 B per level only in the extractor's visitor.
319-- **Choice.** Each file is parsed and extracted on its own scoped thread with 8 MiB plus 4 KiB per source byte (12 KiB unoptimized), over twice the worst measured cost per byte. Every recursion level consumes at least one byte, so no input can overflow it. A file needing more than 1 GiB, longer than 254 KiB optimized or 85 KiB unoptimized, is degraded without parsing. A panic or a thread that cannot start also degrades. The 256-deep bracket scan stays as an early exit, now skipping regex literals (told from division by the preceding character) and ending quoted strings at a line break.
320-- **Cost.** A file under the limit that fools the scan is the worst case: 250,000 unclosed `[` after `return /'/` touches 424 MiB of stack and takes 0.08 s. Of 23,222 local TS/JS source files, 131 (0.56%) exceed 254 KiB, nearly all bundler output. A thread per file moved a whole-repo search from 0.11 s to 0.13 s with identica

